WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.040 --> 00:00:02.169
Good morning John, and welcome to this
special edition of Vlogbrothers where

00:00:02.169 --> 00:00:05.810
we discuss the basics of the United
States Tax Policy and why Warren Buffett

00:00:05.810 --> 00:00:07.180
pays a lower tax rate

00:00:07.180 --> 00:00:10.550
than his secretary. According to the United States government, there are basically

00:00:10.550 --> 00:00:11.919
three ways to make money.

00:00:11.929 --> 00:00:16.100
One: You can inherit it when someone
dies. Two: You can make it

00:00:16.100 --> 00:00:20.020
by working. This is called ordinary
income.

00:00:20.020 --> 00:00:24.470
Making money off YouTube ads, getting paid
to do a job, making raw materials like

00:00:24.470 --> 00:00:28.980
yarn and then converting something worth more
than those raw materials like a cool TFiOS hat.

00:00:28.980 --> 00:00:31.840
That's all ordinary income.
And number three

00:00:31.840 --> 00:00:35.450
capital gains. Now before we talk about
capital gains, let's first talk about the

00:00:35.450 --> 00:00:38.980
ordinary income tax. Some people have a
misconception here that when you reach

00:00:38.980 --> 00:00:42.719
the new tax bracket, all of your income
lumps over into this new place, so you have

00:00:42.719 --> 00:00:46.030
to stay below a certain tax bracket.
That's not how it works. It's actually a

00:00:46.030 --> 00:00:47.489
significantly better system than that.

00:00:47.489 --> 00:00:50.940
Here we have the four lowest tax brackets
for a married couple. Now if you make ten

00:00:50.940 --> 00:00:53.760
thousand dollars a year, you pay a 10
percent tax.

00:00:53.760 --> 00:00:57.160
You probably pay less than that because
of deductions but that's your tax rate

00:00:57.160 --> 00:01:00.059
because the first tax bracket is zero to
seventeen thousand dollars.

00:01:00.059 --> 00:01:03.010
Now if a married couple makes fifty thousand
dollars they've moved into the second

00:01:03.010 --> 00:01:04.479
tax bracket, but still

00:01:04.479 --> 00:01:08.010
their first seventeen thousand dollars
is taxed in the first tax bracket and

00:01:08.010 --> 00:01:11.170
then the rest is taxed in the second.
This works on a scale for a hundred

00:01:11.170 --> 00:01:14.490
thousand dollars and when I made this
graph, I had to make it extra long to fit

00:01:14.490 --> 00:01:18.009
on five hundred thousand dollars which
includes all of the tax brackets, but even

00:01:18.009 --> 00:01:20.540
that person the makes five hundred
thousand dollars still pays

00:01:20.540 --> 00:01:24.610
into all of the lower tax brackets before
they get to their big high up ones.

00:01:24.610 --> 00:01:25.770
Just wanted to clear that up.

00:01:25.770 --> 00:01:29.280
Now, back to capital gains. Capital gains,
is money that you get

00:01:29.280 --> 00:01:33.880
when you buy something and then you wait
and then you sell it later for more.

00:01:33.880 --> 00:01:37.240
That's income, you've made money there.
Most capital gains are made in the stock market

00:01:37.240 --> 00:01:40.560
though you can also do it lots other ways, real
estate being a big one. If you make more than

00:01:40.560 --> 00:01:43.240
like thirty thousand dollars a year, your
capital gains tax

00:01:43.240 --> 00:01:46.470
is 15 percent. It's a flat fifteen
percent for everybody.

00:01:46.470 --> 00:01:49.079
And basically, capital gains is how really rich people make most of their money.

00:01:49.079 --> 00:01:50.009
They invest in stuff

00:01:50.009 --> 00:01:53.390
and then it gets more valuable and then
they sell it. Now, that's basically explaining how it

00:01:53.390 --> 00:01:53.770
works.

00:01:53.770 --> 00:01:58.250
Now I am going to get into how I feel about it. This is
just my opinion, but this has always seemed

00:01:58.250 --> 00:01:59.020
really weird to me.

00:01:59.020 --> 00:02:02.450
Money in the stock market isn't actually doing
anything. Companies don't have access to,

00:02:02.450 --> 00:02:03.540
like, do stuff with that. It's not

00:02:03.540 --> 00:02:06.740
being used to build cars go to Mars or
make video games or whatever.

00:02:06.740 --> 00:02:10.280
I know that investment is important for our
economy, but so is income. Income is,

00:02:10.280 --> 00:02:14.140
to me, it seems like it's worth more. So why
is it then that we tax people almost

00:02:14.140 --> 00:02:15.540
invariably more

00:02:15.540 --> 00:02:18.800
on the money that they earn by
providing actual value

00:02:18.800 --> 00:02:21.650
like proportionate to the amount of
money they make? The idea is that

00:02:21.650 --> 00:02:24.780
investment should be really good for the
economy and that you need to encourage

00:02:24.780 --> 00:02:28.610
people to invest and so you should tax it
less. The problem with that is that there isn't

00:02:28.610 --> 00:02:32.420
a lot a good data that actually supports
that claim. What kind of concerns me is that

00:02:32.420 --> 00:02:35.550
the people who are advising the
government on these tax policy decisions

00:02:35.550 --> 00:02:39.410
are people who make their money this way.
And maybe they, just like a lot of us do,

00:02:39.410 --> 00:02:43.250
overvalue their particular impact on
the American endeavor. Or maybe they're

00:02:43.250 --> 00:02:46.550
super greedy or maybe on the other hand
they're right. Maybe they're right. Maybe

00:02:46.550 --> 00:02:50.319
lower taxes for them is better for all
of us but I can't help but feeling deep

00:02:50.319 --> 00:02:50.860
down

00:02:50.860 --> 00:02:54.239
that it's tremendously unfair that a
waitress at Applebee's pays a higher tax

00:02:54.239 --> 00:02:55.390
rate than a billionaire.

00:02:55.390 --> 00:02:58.690
Whether or not that's good policy, I'm
not sure, but it does seem like bad

00:02:58.690 --> 00:03:02.150
ethics. But as I say, I am not an expert and
I'm completely willing to be convinced

00:03:02.150 --> 00:03:05.280
that I'm wrong. Nerfighteria, you are not
wrong, because you kicked

